1.1.text:
Article 33

(1) The community may participate in women's protection.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals and/or organizations.

(3) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) may be carried out by:

a. providing input and suggestions to the District Government regarding policies, programs and activities for women's protection;
b. monitoring District Government policies related to women's protection;
c. initiating or proposing new policies that need to be established by the District Government in efforts to optimize women's protection;
d. assisting gampong apparatus in order to implement women's protection efforts as regulated in this Qanun;
e. conducting socialization about the elimination of violence against women;
f. providing information and/or reporting violence against women to law enforcement institutions or authorities; and
g. monitoring the performance of law enforcement officials in handling cases of violence against women. h

Article 3

Protection of women has the objectives to:

a. strengthen regional policy and budget support in the framework of optimizing the implementation of protection of women;
b. reinforce the commitment of policy makers at the regency, sub-district and gampong levels in the framework of enhancing efforts and guarantees of protection for women;
c. enhance coordination, integration, synchronization, and synergy between SKPK with all stakeholders including religious organizations, professional organizations, academics, private sector and the wider community in efforts to improve the quality of protection of women;
d. build community knowledge and awareness to support efforts to protect women;
e. improve the institutional capacity and human resources of UPTD PPA;
f. provide guarantees to women to be able to fulfill their rights as human beings in all aspects of life;
g. provide a sense of security to women in all aspects of life; and
h. reduce the rate of violence against women.

Article 25

(1) The Regency Government has obligations and responsibilities to carry out efforts to prevent violence against women in the form of:

a. collecting data and information about victims of violence and laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values against women;
c. socializing laws and regulations related to the provision of protection for victims of violence;
d. fostering support from religious leaders and traditional leaders to build public awareness of the importance of protecting women;
e. monitoring and evaluating the provision of protection for victims of violence; and
f. preparing reports on the provision of protection for women victims of violence.
(2) To anticipate acts of violence against women, the Regency Government has the obligation to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:
a. establishing institutions and facilitating the provision of integrated services for victims involving community elements; and b. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protecting victims.
(3) To prevent acts of violence against women, the Regency Government carries out the following efforts:
a. conducting socialization regarding regulations and policies relating to violence against women for gampong apparatus, traditional leaders and religious leaders;
b. providing counseling to immediately report to local authorities if violence against women occurs;
c. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence; and d. other efforts deemed strategic to prevent violence against women.

I

Article 9

Implementation of protection of women is provided to:

a. women as housewives;
b. women heads of household;
c. women domestic workers;
d. women workers/laborers;
e. women with disabilities;
f. women homeless;
g. women former inmates of correctional institutions;
h. women victims of violence;
i. Women victims of conflict; and
j. elderly women.

Article 35

(1) The District Government encourages the business sector to participate in the protection of women from acts of violence.

(2) Such participation is carried out both internally and externally.

(3) Internal participation as referred to in paragraph (2) may take the form of: a. socialization to employees within the company regarding the dangers of acts of violence against women;

b. building an internal women's system to handle issues of violence against women; and

c. providing social assistance as a form of corporate social responsibility.

(4) External participation as referred to in paragraph (2) may take the form of: a. providing special opportunities for women victims of violence to work in the company;

b. creating special education and training for women victims of violence;

c. educational assistance for women victims of violence who are withdrawn from education; and

d. fostering and enhancing the economic independence of women victims of violence.

Article 7

Every woman victim of violence is entitled to obtain:

a. respect for dignity and worth as a human being; b. health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim; c. determine her own decision in handling the violence case she experienced;
d. obtain information;
e. obtain confidentiality guarantee;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. complaint handling;
h. obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. assistance; and
j. obtain empowerment after her case is resolved.

Article 28

(1) The Gampong Government supports women's protection efforts by:

a. issuing gampong-level regulations on women's protection;
b. socializing regulations related to women's empowerment and protection to the community;
c. encouraging the creation of a gampong environment that is friendly to women;
d. building community awareness including traditional leaders and religious leaders at the gampong level to care about issues of violence against women;
e. allocating budget to support women's empowerment and protection programs; and
f. building a system for handling violence against women at the gampong level.
(2) The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, above can be developed in accordance with the gampong's financial capacity, problems and needs of women. k

Article 5

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and maintain her life, and improve the level of welfare in her life;
b. practice religious teachings in accordance with Islamic sharia;
c. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
d. obtain protection of her personal self, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
e. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue her lineage;

I

f. obtain fair and equal treatment in carrying out her roles and functions both in family life, society and fully participate in every process of making, determining and implementing development policies;

g. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels, and pathways of education, without any obstacles or pressure in order to improve her skills and quality of life;

h. obtain information and knowledge for women about health, especially comprehensive reproductive health; and i. obtain special protection in carrying out work or profession against matters that threaten her safety and/or health concerning reproductive function.

(2) Every woman in her capacity as a wife, while in a marital bond, has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters concerning her marital life, relationship with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

(3) In addition to the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), a woman as a wife is entitled to receive material and spiritual support from her husband.

(4) After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities from her former husband over all matters concerning children, taking into account the best interests of the child.

Article 38

(1) The Regent through the Regional Apparatus that manages the affairs of women's empowerment conducts supervision and monitoring of the protection of women.

(2) The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) is as follows:

a. providing socialization to the community and business sector regarding policies on the protection of women;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding the protection of women, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c. facilitating capacity building of institutions organizing the protection of women;
d. facilitating forums for women's participation, women's organizations and organizations for persons with disabilities;
e. facilitating rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for the protection of women; and g. granting awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have made efforts to protect women.

Article 16

(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women as referred to in Article 9 letter f, in the framework of providing protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.

(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection over:

a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunity to obtain education and skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 24

The Regent in implementing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women by making efforts to:

a. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women among regency apparatus;
b. cooperate with Regencies/Cities within one Province, and cooperation among Regencies/Cities in other Provinces in the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. strengthen institutional capacity for the implementation of policies, programs and activities for the protection of women;
e. facilitate services for the protection of women;
f. provide services for the protection of women;
g. implement affirmative action for the protection of women; and
h. prepare a data system for the protection of women.

